# Secrets — never commit. The wildcard matters: ".env" alone does NOT match
# ".env.backup2", which is how a Supabase key reached a commit and tripped
# GitHub secret scanning. Nothing named .env* is published, template included.
.env
.env*

# Data: the bulk is ignored, but a committed sample is required by the
# assignment (the website must link to real raw and clean data).
raw/*
!raw/sample/
# The full gzipped archives stay OUT of the repository. Responses captured
# before credential redaction was added carry the API key inside the response
# "url" field, so the archives are not safe to publish. The website links to
# redacted sample responses in raw/sample/ and to the complete parsed raw
# table in exports/events_raw.csv instead.
# Nothing under data/ is published. The Bandsintown-era sample CSVs that used
# to live in data/sample/ carried an app_id inside a URL column.
data/

# exports/ IS committed: the assignment requires the website to link to the
# raw and the cleaned data, so these CSVs have to be public.

# Python — note the wildcard: a stray ".venv-1" must be ignored too, or a
# whole virtualenv ends up staged for commit.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv*/
venv*/
env/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store

# Local safety copies and files that are not part of the deliverable
backups/
notebooks/

# Files delivered into the chat, not part of the project
"Claude outputs/"
Claude outputs/
